# Which shops require a new customer to be APPROVED before they can buy.
#
# One shop key per line. A shop listed here announces a new signup to the address
# in its New Order Notifications field, and its customers see the approval
# wording. A shop that is not listed sends neither.
#
# ── why this file exists, in the client's own words ─────────────────────────
#
# The first version of this guard had no file. It asked whether the shop had any
# customer groups at all, on the reasoning that approval on this setup IS a
# customer group: the admin approves somebody by moving them into one. That was
# true of the estate as it stood, and it was measured: the wholesale shop had 3
# groups and every retail shop had 0.
#
# He read it and said what was wrong with it before it could bite:
#
#   "I am thinking about how our retail shops will be used very soon when orders
#    start to move. I can see us using the customer groups for things like VIP,
#    Loyalty, Staff, etc which would trigger the email, or have you setup a
#    customer_approval_required = true / false?"
#
# He is right. The day he adds a VIP group to a retail shop, that shop starts
# announcing every retail signup as waiting for approval, which is the exact fault
# he reported on 1 September and which the guard was written to stop. A group is
# how approval is RECORDED; it is not what decides whether a shop does approvals.
#
# So it is declared, per shop, and nothing is inferred.
#
# ── the shape ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   <shop>            one per line, comments and blank lines ignored
#
# Matched against SHOP_KEY first and then SHOP, the same order every other
# per-shop lookup uses, so a shop whose deployment name differs from its content
# key can be named by either.
#
# Tracked rather than gitignored: a shop key is not client data, and a policy that
# lives on one laptop is a policy that comes off the next time somebody else
# deploys. Same reasoning as config/gated_shops.

# The New Zealand wholesale shop. Trade accounts are approved by hand before they
# see trade pricing.
b2b

# The commercial shop on the other server. Prices are hidden from guests by its
# channel, so a customer who has not been approved cannot see a figure anywhere,
# and nobody would know they were waiting without this.
#
# Named by its SHOP_KEY, not by its deployment name: this repository is public and
# the deployment name carries his brand. That is safe rather than fragile, because
# a deployment that forgets SHOP_KEY does not boot at all: config/initializers/
# shop_locale.rb cannot find its wording file and raises. So the failure mode of a
# missing key is a container that will not start, not a shop that quietly stops
# telling anyone that somebody is waiting.
business
